## Review — verified independently, holding approval on the PR body only

Kam, this is a good fix and the reasoning in it holds up. I re-measured everything from scratch on a
second machine rather than reading your numbers back to you, and the substance stands. I'm holding
approval on one clerical thing and one number that doesn't match — neither is about the code.

### The defect reproduces, exactly as you described it

Independently, with no repo code in the loop — a bare `https.request` probe:

```
CRLF             THREW    ERR_INVALID_CHAR: Invalid character in header content ["X-Probe"]
non-latin1       THREW    ERR_INVALID_CHAR: Invalid character in header content ["X-Probe"]
NUL              THREW    ERR_INVALID_CHAR: Invalid character in header content ["X-Probe"]
CLEAN(control)   RETURNED (no sync throw)
```

And the call site is exposed as you said: `services/originate/src/routes/webhooks.ts:450` has no
`try/catch` around the call, while the m365 loop still has its own at `index.ts:1263`. So the
asymmetry you found is real.

### Red → green, reproduced rather than taken on trust

Two trees: your head `7d8a3f0e4`, and the base guard `ff5218867` with your test file copied over
**byte-identical** (`diff -q` clean), so only the guard differs.

```
head      Test Files  1 passed (1)    Tests  10 passed (10)
redproof  Test Files  1 failed (1)    Tests   3 failed | 7 passed (10)
          × CRLF   × non-latin1   × NUL
          TypeError: Invalid character in header content ["X-Probe"]
            at src/security/ssrf-guard.ts:492:21   (const req = mod.request()
```

Exactly the three unencodable cells red, the control green. The control is doing real work — pinned
by message (`/deadline/`, asserted *not* to match `/Invalid character/`), so it can't pass on "some
failure". That's the part that makes this a proof rather than a green.

### Everything else I ran

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| `packages/shared` vitest — head `7d8a3f0e4` | **791 passed / 41 files**, 0 failed |
| `packages/shared` vitest — base `ff5218867` | **787 passed / 41 files** |
| `packages/shared` vitest — your two files onto **current `develop`** | **791 passed / 41 files**, 0 failed |
| `ks914-shipped-path.test.ts` — cells, base → head | **6 → 10** |
| `services/originate` jest — KS-914 call-site cells | **4 passed / 4** |
| `eslint` on both changed files | **0 errors, 1 warning** — and that warning (`prefer-const` on `deadline`) is byte-identical on the base, so it isn't yours |
| `tsc --noEmit -p packages/shared/tsconfig.json` | **exit 0** |

Host caveat, so you can weigh it: arm64 Mac, Node v24.18.0, no stack running. `preflight.sh` I did
*not* re-run — several legs want a live gateway and a fresh install, and against a detached
extraction the results would be noise rather than evidence. I'm taking your 12 legs as read.

### Could you double-check two figures?

The head total agrees exactly. The other two don't, and I'd rather you settle it than me:

| | PR body | what I measured |
|---|---|---|
| base `ff5218867` | 784 | **787 passed / 41 files** |
| head `7d8a3f0e4` | 791 | 791 — agrees |
| cells added | +7 | **+4** (`ks914-shipped-path.test.ts` 6 → 10, which is the 3 `it.each` rows plus the control) |

It may well be my extraction rather than your run — I built the base tree with `git archive` and
linked `node_modules` in, so a stale build artefact on either side could explain it. Worth one
re-run on your machine either way, since 787 + 4 and 784 + 7 both land on 791 and only one of them
is true.

### Why I haven't approved yet

Only this: the PR description doesn't carry the template. There's no `## Linear` section with the
ticket URL, and neither pre-merge acknowledgment checkbox
(`ack:schemathesis` / `ack:akto`) is present — the template's own header note asks that block be
kept on every PR, and `pr-premerge-ack.yml` parses those two markers. The Linear linkback comment
and `Closes KS-931` cover the traceability in practice, so this is form rather than substance, but
with the ack boxes gone there's nothing recording that the suites were considered.

I'd suggest restoring the block with **both boxes unticked and a line under each saying why the
suite doesn't apply here** — no route, no spec, no schema, no endpoint — rather than deleting the
section. That way the gate stays visible and the "not applicable" is a stated decision rather than a
blank. Same for the PII block: an explicit "not applicable" reads better than empty boxes. I've
drafted a full replacement body and can drop it here or hand it over, whichever you prefer.

Once that's in and the counts are settled, I'm happy to approve.

### Two small observations, entirely your call

**The `write`/`end` wrap looks unreachable given the declared type.** I went looking for a test for
that branch and couldn't construct one, so I measured what actually throws there — Node v24.18.0:

```
write+end  lone surrogate   RETURNED (no sync throw)
write+end  NUL in body      RETURNED (no sync throw)
write+end  non-latin1       RETURNED (no sync throw)
write+end  CRLF             RETURNED (no sync throw)
write+end  1MB string       RETURNED (no sync throw)
after destroy()             RETURNED (no sync throw)
non-string body             THREW    ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE
```

Header *content* is latin-1 validated — which is exactly why your header cells throw — but bodies
are utf-8 encoded, so the same characters go through fine. And `write`/`end` after `destroy()`
returns rather than throwing, so the comment's second case doesn't arise either (the deadline is
async, so it couldn't have fired before those synchronous lines in any event). The only synchronous
throw I could produce is a non-string body, which `SafeOutboundInit`'s `body?: string` rules out.

The wrap costs nothing and I'd not ask you to remove it — belt and braces on a contract this
load-bearing seems fair. It's the two reasons in the comment above it I'd gently flag, since the
rest of this PR is careful to measure rather than assert.

**Branch is 111 commits behind `develop`.** Inert, as far as I can tell — both files you touch are
byte-identical on `develop` and on your base, and I ran the suite as-if-merged onto current
`develop`: 791 / 41, clean. So no re-measurement needed, just noting it against CONTRIBUTING's
"up to date with base" line.

### For the record, on what isn't here

I agree with not running the platform suites, and I'd have made the same call. Your own reachability
note on KS-931 is the reason — `POST /api/webhooks/:id/test` hard-codes its payload and
`dispatchEvent` has no product call sites, so there's no live path that would exercise this. A
stack sweep would have produced a green that proved nothing about the change. The vitest cells are
the right gate and they're honest ones.

Likewise the KS-933 note in your evidence block — flagging that `src/__tests__` sits outside the
type gate, rather than quietly benefiting from the ambiguity, is the right way round.

Nice piece of work. It's a small diff that closes a real gap between what the module says and what
it does, and the control cell is what makes it stick.

